{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310709,"nonce":"STfA6umqWuBumGh1Mq8vXfIdIuw=","title":"`static inline var FOO:Int = 0xLIT` was reading garbage when referenced cross-context — caused by stale TAST SymbolIds missing in ...","message":"**Status: FIXED (commit on `main`, post f78eaad).** 128/128 haxe regression\nstill green; llama-chat now reaches Q4_K_M decode (separate bug,\n[[bugs-gguf-tensor-decode-crash]]).\n\n\n`static inline var` constants like `GGUFReader.MAGIC = 0x46554747` get registered\nin the per-`TastToHirContext` `inline_var_values: BTreeMap\u003cSymbolId, HirLiteral\u003e`.\nWhen the same file (e.g. `GGUFReader`) appears in a large import graph, the file\nends up being lowered to HIR more than once and **different `TastToHirContext`s\nallocate different `SymbolId`s for the same field**. On the second lowering,\nthe TAST nodes still carry the original SymbolId (4365) but\n`seed_inline_vars_from_global` populated the fresh map with the new SymbolId\n(6697). The lookup at the HIR `Variable` arm was *SymbolId-only*, so it missed\nand fell through to `HirExprKind::Variable`. Then MIR's Variable handler hit\nthe global-symbol name-fallback (`hir_to_mir.rs:8499`) and emitted\n`LoadGlobal(global_id=0)` — pointing at an uninitialized slot, hence the\nruntime garbage (0x62DD0560, 0xBC272FE0, …).\n\nConfirmed by probe trace:\n```\n[HIR-VAR-INLINE] symbol_id=4365 literal=Int(1179993927)       ← first pass: hit\n[HIR-VAR-MISS]   symbol_id=4365 NOT in map (size=2)            ← second pass: miss\n                  map=[MAGIC@SymbolId(6697), ALIGNMENT_DEFAULT@SymbolId(6698)]\n[hir_to_mir:8499] name=MAGIC gid=IrGlobalId(0)                 ← LoadGlobal emitted\n```\n\n\n`tast_to_hir.rs` Variable arm (line ~1498) now does a name + qualified-name\nfallback when the SymbolId lookup misses — same pattern as the existing\n`StaticFieldAccess` arm at line ~1666. If a same-name candidate exists with a\nmatching qualified name (when both sides have one), or a same-name candidate\nexists at all (when qualified info is absent), the inline literal is returned.\n\nThis is purely additive — the direct SymbolId lookup path is untouched.\n\n\nThat's the deeper fix (one canonical SymbolId for a given qualified name across\ncontexts), but it touches the BLADE save/load + symbol-table-restore machinery\nand would ripple through dozens of cross-context name-fallback paths. The HIR-\nside fallback is narrowly scoped, follows the established pattern in\n`StaticFieldAccess`, and is exactly what was needed to surface the constant.\n\n\n- Any other map keyed by `SymbolId` that needs to survive cross-context\n  lowering should consider a name-based fallback. Candidates: `field_index_map`,\n  `function_id_for_symbol`, `property_access_map`. Most already have one but\n  worth checking when a \"works standalone, breaks under heavy imports\" symptom\n  shows up again.\n- `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` + `RAYZOR_DUMP_CLIF=\u003cname\u003e` (added in `f78eaad`)\n  are the right tools to spot this class of bug — CLIF showing\n  `rayzor_global_load(0)` for an operand that should be a literal is the tell.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_static_inline_var_cross_context` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}